:db/id 17592186328320 :question/worked-solution "The function $f(x,y)=\\sqrt{1+x^2y^2}$ is continuous on the rectangle $D=[0,\\sqrt2]\\times[-\\sqrt3,2]$. Fubini's theorem gives both I and II directly.\n\nIn III, the intervals are assigned to the opposite coordinate variables. Its region is the reflected rectangle $D^*=[-\\sqrt3,2]\\times[0,\\sqrt2]$, rather than $D$. Nevertheless, the integral has the same value: set $u=y$ and $v=x$, whose absolute Jacobian is $1$. Because $f(v,u)=f(u,v)$,\n\n$$\\int_{-\\sqrt3}^{2}\\int_0^{\\sqrt2}\\sqrt{1+x^2y^2}\\,dy\\,dx\n=\\int_{-\\sqrt3}^{2}\\int_0^{\\sqrt2}\\sqrt{1+u^2v^2}\\,du\\,dv\n=\\iint_D\\sqrt{1+x^2y^2}\\,dA.$$\n\nThus I, II, and III are all true. The original source's answer \"I and II only\" identifies the two expressions whose bounds directly match the stated coordinates, but omits the equal value in III due to symmetry. The study answer is \"I, II, and III\"." :db/ensure [:question/validate] :question/answer-fields ["q-72175/mathematical-correction-v1/selection"]}]
